{"subjectId":"sql","id":"pivoting","title":"Pivoting Rows into Columns","group":"Interview Prep","order":60,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A pivot turns row values (subjects, months, statuses) into columns. The portable way is <strong>conditional aggregation</strong>: <code>GROUP BY</code> the row key and one <code>CASE</code> per output column.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT * FROM exam_scores ORDER BY student, subject;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>student | subject | score\n--------+---------+------\nAsha    | English | 78\nAsha    | Math    | 91\nAsha    | Science | 84\nMeera   | English | 95\nMeera   | Math    | 75\nRavi    | Math    | 67\nRavi    | Science | 88</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-table-column\"></i>Pivot with CASE</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT student,\n       MAX(CASE WHEN subject = 'Math'    THEN score END) AS math,\n       MAX(CASE WHEN subject = 'Science' THEN score END) AS science,\n       MAX(CASE WHEN subject = 'English' THEN score END) AS english,\n       SUM(score) AS total\nFROM exam_scores\nGROUP BY student\nORDER BY student;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>student | math | science | english | total\n--------+------+---------+---------+------\nAsha    | 91   | 84      | 78      | 253\nMeera   | 75   | NULL    | 95      | 170\nRavi    | 67   | 88      | NULL    | 155</code></pre></figure>\n<p><code>MAX</code> just picks the single value in each cell (any aggregate works when there is one row per cell; use <code>SUM</code> when several rows should add up). Missing combinations become <code>NULL</code>.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-calendar-stats\"></i>Business pivot: order counts per month and status</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT strftime('%Y-%m', order_date) AS month,\n       SUM(status = 'delivered') AS delivered,\n       SUM(status = 'shipped')   AS shipped,\n       SUM(status = 'pending')   AS pending,\n       SUM(status = 'cancelled') AS cancelled,\n       COUNT(*)                  AS total\nFROM orders\nGROUP BY month\nORDER BY month;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>month   | delivered | shipped | pending | cancelled | total\n--------+-----------+---------+---------+-----------+------\n2024-01 | 2         | 0       | 0       | 0         | 2\n2024-02 | 1         | 1       | 0       | 1         | 3\n2024-03 | 1         | 1       | 1       | 0         | 3</code></pre></figure>\n<p>In SQLite and MySQL a comparison is <code>1</code>/<code>0</code>, so <code>SUM(status = 'x')</code> counts matches. Portable form: <code>SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'x' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)</code>; PostgreSQL: <code>COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE status = 'x')</code>.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-table-row\"></i>Unpivot: columns back into rows</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>CREATE TABLE quarterly (region TEXT, q1 INTEGER, q2 INTEGER, q3 INTEGER);\nINSERT INTO quarterly VALUES ('North', 100, 120, 90), ('South', 80, 95, 110);\n\nSELECT region, 'Q1' AS quarter, q1 AS sales FROM quarterly\nUNION ALL SELECT region, 'Q2', q2 FROM quarterly\nUNION ALL SELECT region, 'Q3', q3 FROM quarterly\nORDER BY region, quarter;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>region | quarter | sales\n-------+---------+------\nNorth  | Q1      | 100\nNorth  | Q2      | 120\nNorth  | Q3      | 90\nSouth  | Q1      | 80\nSouth  | Q2      | 95\nSouth  | Q3      | 110</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL Server / Oracle (PIVOT / UNPIVOT)</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT *\nFROM (SELECT student, subject, score FROM exam_scores) src\nPIVOT (MAX(score) FOR subject IN ([Math], [Science], [English])) p;\n\nSELECT region, quarter, sales\nFROM quarterly\nUNPIVOT (sales FOR quarter IN (q1, q2, q3)) u;</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>SQL needs the output columns known when the query is written. For a <em>dynamic</em> list of pivot columns you must generate the SQL text in application code (or dynamic SQL) — or pivot in the reporting tool.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you convert rows to columns in SQL?</summary><div><p>Conditional aggregation: <code>SELECT key, MAX(CASE WHEN attr = 'A' THEN value END) AS a, … FROM t GROUP BY key</code>. It works in every engine; SQL Server/Oracle also have <code>PIVOT</code>, PostgreSQL has <code>crosstab()</code> in the <code>tablefunc</code> extension.</p></div></details></section>"}